#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287825 :question/problem "Given that $M=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & p & 0 \\\\ p-3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $det(M)=-23,$ find all the possible values of $p.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & p & 0 \\\\ p-3 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}p & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ p-3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & p \\\\ p-3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+3\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (3p-{p}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3-2{p}^{2}+6p \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+6p-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-23,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{p}^{2}+6p-3 & =-23 \\\\ 2{p}^{2}-6p-20 & =0 \\\\ {p}^{2}-3p-10 & =0 \\\\ p & =\\frac{-(-3)\\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-10)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{49}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm 7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions are $p=-2$ and $p=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287850 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-2,0,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{d}=⟨4,-1,0⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{c}$ onto $\\mathbf{d}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{d}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\cdot 4+0\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{17}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287886 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{r}=⟨1,-2,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{t}=⟨0,1,-2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}\\times \\mathbf{t} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r}\\times \\mathbf{t}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287887 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,LMN$, if $L(0,0,1)$, $M(2,1,1)$, $N(3,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =\\mathbf{m}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨3,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287896 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-2,0,1⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨5,2,-4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨1,0,3⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(2\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot 0)-0+(5\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-12-0-2 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287897 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})$, if $4\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=12.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =12 \\\\ 4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =12 \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287909 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{g}=\\begin{bmatrix}{r}^{2} \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{h}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ {r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $\\mathbf{g}⊥\\mathbf{h}$ and $r<0$, find the value of $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{g}⊥\\mathbf{h}$ ($\\mathbf{g}$ and $\\mathbf{h}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{g}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{g}\\cdot \\mathbf{h} & ={g}_{x}\\cdot {h}_{x}+{g}_{y}\\cdot {h}_{y}+{g}_{z}\\cdot {h}_{z} \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot {r}^{2}+(-6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3{r}^{2}-{r}^{2}-12 \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{g}\\cdot \\mathbf{h} & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =6 \\\\ r & =\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $r<0.$ Therefore, $r=-\\sqrt{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287928 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨3,2,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨3,4,1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{26}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287929 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨4,1,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨2,3,3⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{\\sqrt{22}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287930 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,1,-1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,4,2⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{b}\\cdot \\mathbf{a}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b} & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}-4\\sqrt{21}}{\\sqrt{126}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287931 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{c}=⟨4,3,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{d}=⟨2,1,2⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{c}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{d}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{d}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{c}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{d} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{c}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{d} & =\\frac{11}{3}-\\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =11(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =11(\\frac{5}{15}-\\frac{3}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287932 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-1,2,\\sqrt{11}⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨4,3,\\sqrt{11}⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 3+\\sqrt{11}\\cdot \\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(\\sqrt{11})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{b}\\cdot \\mathbf{a}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(\\sqrt{11})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b} & =\\frac{13}{6}-\\frac{13}{4} \\\\  & =13(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =13(\\frac{2}{12}-\\frac{3}{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287933 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,4,2\\sqrt{2}⟩$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨1,1,\\sqrt{2}⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}+{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{p}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1+2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{p}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}+{p}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}+{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{p}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q} & =\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{9}{5} \\\\  & =9(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =9(\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{2}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287934 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =(2x)({e}^{x})+({x}^{2})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x+2)x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287942 :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}tan⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}tan⁡x)^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot tan⁡x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (tan⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(tan⁡x)+({e}^{x})({sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287943 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=cos⁡xlog⁡x,$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=cos⁡x$ and $v(x)=log⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(cos⁡x)}^{′}log⁡x+cos⁡x{(log⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =(-sin⁡x)(log⁡x)+(cos⁡x)\\left(\\frac{1}{xln⁡10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{cos⁡x}{xln⁡10}-sin⁡xlog⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287957 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,3,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨6,4,0⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 0-6\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 4-6\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0)\\mathbf{i}-(0)\\mathbf{j}+(8-18)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287958 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨0,0,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,0,1⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((0)(1)-(0)(3))\\mathbf{i}-((0)(1)-(2)(3))\\mathbf{j}+((0)(0)-(2)(0))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-0)\\mathbf{i}-(0-6)\\mathbf{j}+(0-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287959 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨x,2,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2x,5,2⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{393}.$ Find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2x & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 2x & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4-15)\\mathbf{i}-(2x-6x)\\mathbf{j}+(5x-4x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-11,4x,x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-11)^{2}+(4x)^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121+16{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+121}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{393} \\\\ \\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+121} & =\\sqrt{393} \\\\ 17{x}^{2}+121 & =393 \\\\ 17{x}^{2} & =272 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287960 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,5,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,3,0⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((5)(0)-(3)(4))\\mathbf{i}-((1)(0)-(2)(4))\\mathbf{j}+((1)(3)-(2)(5))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-12)\\mathbf{i}-(0-8)\\mathbf{j}+(3-10)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-12\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}-7\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287961 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-2,2,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((2)(-3)-(2)(0))\\mathbf{i}-((-2)(-3)-(0)(0))\\mathbf{j}+((-2)(2)-(0)(2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-6-0)\\mathbf{i}-(6-0)\\mathbf{j}+(-4-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-6\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287968 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf{u}=⟨2,1,-1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨1,2,0⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287969 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf{f}=⟨1,1,-2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{g}=⟨3,2,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =5\\mathbf{i}-7\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨5,-7,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-7)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287970 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf{h}=⟨2,0,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{l}=⟨4,1,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{h}\\times \\mathbf{l} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,6,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{h}\\times \\mathbf{l}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287971 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,RST$, if $R(3,-2,1)$, $S(2,-2,2)$, $T(1,-3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS} & =\\mathbf{s}-\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =\\mathbf{t}-\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,RST$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287972 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,3,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨3,4,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,0,1⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)-3(3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1)+2(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =4-3-16 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287973 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,-1,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,0,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,4,2⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 3)+(2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 3)+2(2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-12-2+16 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287974 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})$, if $3\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (2\\mathbf{b}\\times (-\\mathbf{c}))=8.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (2\\mathbf{b}\\times (-\\mathbf{c})) & =8 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =8 \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287975 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})$, if $3\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}\\mathbf{b}\\times 3\\mathbf{c})=18\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}\\mathbf{b}\\times 3\\mathbf{c}) & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287976 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})$, if $3\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (6\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=90.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (6\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =90 \\\\ 3\\cdot 6\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =90 \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287977 :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa1c1c3ae2769f45854de4f0b00fc394fccb40b40704cfd09e6eb11eee3c07af.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=3{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(3{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(3{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{e}^{2\\theta }+9{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{18{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287979 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(2,1,2)$, $B(3,3,3)$, $C(-2,-1,3)$, and $D(4,1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 1)-2((-4)\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1)+((-4)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =-4+20+4 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|20|=20.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288015 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${x}^{′}(0)=2\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is horizontal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288017 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5,\\,y=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1),\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5))=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288018 :question/problem "![](images/59/59704c6b8e643337113ab99b22eaeaac8d59ac3c15042409e8a0a8eac6ee54fd.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{t}{2},\\,y={e}^{t}\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{({e}^{2t})+\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290260 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b77bdde611689095cdea6a3a08ce9828b9e20040283dc0c251250538a5cfba3.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,y={t}^{2}-1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6t-2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t}{6t-2}=\\frac{t}{3t-1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2}{3(2)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290274 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290298 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290299 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\n$$\nfrom $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290300 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y=t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}+(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
